      "id": "md2025_1559",
      "subset": "S4",
      "task_type": "cross-company",
      "question": "What differences exist between AEP and ED regarding their forward-looking statement disclaimers?",
      "expected_answer": "AEP's disclaimer includes an explicit list of factors that could cause actual results to differ, such as 'Changes in economic conditions, electric market demand and demographic patterns,' 'Inflationary or deflationary interest rate trends,' and 'New legislation.' ED's disclaimer is less enumerated; it states that strategies may not be effective to address changes in the external business environment and lists factors like 'Changes to the competitive landscape, public policy, laws or regulations (or interpretations thereof), customer behavior or technology.' Thus, AEP provides a more detailed enumerated list of risk factors.",
      "predicted_answer": "AEP includes a dedicated forward-looking statement disclaimer in Item 7, identifying signal words like \"expect,\" \"anticipate,\" and \"plan,\" stating these are subject to risks that could cause actual results to differ materially, noting the statements are presented as of the document date, and disclaiming any obligation to update except as required by law, followed by a bulleted list of risk factors [E1][E3]. ED's Item 7 evidence does not contain a forward-looking statement disclaimer; instead, it presents a risk factor about strategy effectiveness and the external business environment, discussing competitive landscape changes, public policy, distributed energy resources, and climate legislation impacts without any meta-disclosure about forward-looking statements [E2].\n\nSources:\n[E1] AEP_2024.html — Item 7, Management's Discussion and Analysis of Financial Condition and Results of Operations\n[E2] ED_2024.html — Item 7, The Companies' Strategies May Not Be Effective To Address Changes In The External Business Environment\n[E3] AEP_2024.html — Item 7, Management's Discussion and Analysis of Financial Condition and Results of Operations",
